 you want to trade  with foreign countries you are treated  suspiciously now the question is how  exactly does the state do it what  mechanisms does the argentine government  use to torpedo international trade well  you see currently the first factor is  capital controls that is in the rules  that the government has set for  argentines who want to buy sell and move  dollars which as you all know is the  reference currency for world trade these  controls have led to two exchange rates  in the gaucho country  on the one hand there is the official  exchange rate which at the time of  making this video was around 104 pesos  per dollar and on the other hand we have  the free exchange rate in the informal  market better known as the blue dollar  exchange rate where to give you an idea  the dollar costs twice as much about 220  pesos per dollar  of course all banking operations are  carried out taking into account the  official dollar which as we're about to  see is not so easy to obtain but let's  start by looking at what happens with  purchases that is imports  [Music]  any company large medium or small that  needs to import products from abroad  must face a long labyrinth of paperwork  and forms that will make it very  difficult to obtain the foreign currency  it needs you see in order to import  products companies have to obtain an lna  a non-automatic import license these are  licenses granted by the argentine state  in a totally discretionary manner in  other words getting one will depend on  whether the official on duty considers  that your request to buy abroad makes  sense or not and so the interested  parties are obliged to explain why and  for what purpose they want the foreign  currency request it well in advance and  pray that the official in charge of the  case will listen to their prayers and  grant them their much needed license if  not tough luck you're basically screwed  in this way the argentine government  controls the volume of imports entering  the country and if suddenly the foreign  exchange of the central bank is reduced  very fast the faucet is closed and many  more licenses than usual are rejected  although this of course means a very  harsh punishment for local companies  that overnight may face the inability to  obtain raw materials on products from  abroad that are necessary for their  activity  bad luck this explains why in argentina  it is common for companies to face  recurring shortages of materials which  for example may prevent them from  improving production or repairing a  machine so you see the official dollar  in argentina is cheap but it comes with  a hell of a catch but wait a minute if  you get along well with the government  voila you can have an inexhaustible  source of wealth why  [Music]  well because by setting an official rate  that has nothing to do with the market  rate what the government is doing is  basically subsidizing your purchases  abroad but i repeat only as long as you  have their blessing yes i know it's a  pretty awful system okay so this is true  for imports but what about exports and  foreign sales well visualpolitik fans if  you export a product and you get dollars  the government is going to forcefully  convert them into pesos at the official  exchange rate of course that is at 104  pesos per dollar and not at 220 which  was the price of the blue dollar at the  time of making this video  the problem is that argentina's economy  in the face of the peso disaster is  largely dollarized and so the cost of  companies are determined in many cases  by the exchange rate of the blue dollar  to put it another way when a company  decides to export its production what it  will receive are pesos and an exchange  rate that is half of what the dollars  that the government takes away from it  are worth in the market  remember that when an argentine company  wants to import something it benefits  from the cheap dollar the problem is  that in addition to having to pay a lot  of tariffs it is not guaranteed to have  access to dollars and when you want to  export well you end up only getting half  crazy or local as they might say this in  addition to taxes labor regulations and  well all kinds of obstacles in the  country explains why despite argentina's  low wage costs there are practically no  multinationals willing to invest in the  country to produce there and then sell  to the rest of the world because of  course given such an environment who  would do it but take note because this  is not the end of the story  [Music]  since these types of policies have  destroyed its industrial sector  argentina is mainly characterized by the  export of agri-food products such as  meat cereals and flour these products  are also consumed by argentines  themselves in order to prevent foreign  buyers from competing with local  consumers argentine companies face  maximum export quotas in other words the  government forces companies to sell a  considerable part of their production of  the local market at prices set by the  government itself this all may seem very  nice very considerate even the problem  though visualpolitik fans is that it  reduces foreign exchange inflows wages  and investment increasing productive  capacity the end result is bad for  everyone but then there's even more for  the last 20 years argentina's star  export has been soya beans a product  that is devoured in china but barely  consumed locally so here the export  quota is meaningless now do you think  this was going to stop the politicians  well if so you are very much mistaken  soybean producers can export as much  soybeans as they want but in return they  pay an additional tax an extraordinary  tax  while the blue dollar which is the free  dollar trades around 220 pesos soybean  exporters barely receive 70 pesos for  each dollar they export this is because  the government first converts the  dollars into pesos at the official  exchange rate and then imposes an  additional tax of 33  from 220 to 70 pesos per dollar exported  and take a deep breath because we're  still not done yet as if all of this  were not enough the government keeps  approving new barriers to make it  increasingly difficult to sell abroad  for example aggro exporters must be  registered in the so-called registry of  operators in the agri-food chain  something that forces them to deal with  a lot of paperwork for example they have  to make statements about the sales they  plan to make in the coming year submit  financial information to prove they are  solvent report in detail on their  employers and the salaries they pay  verify that the companies they interact  with also comply with the argentine  government's requirements and so on and  so forth in short it's a complete  nightmare  and then there's even more once it has  dealt with the government any company  that wants to export has to deal with  the control of two powerful unions that  of the truck drivers led by the moneo  clan and that of the port employees  suffice to say that the intervention of  these unions makes transportation in  argentina particularly expensive  competition limited and schedules and  service a disaster on top of that if you  don't get along with unions or you don't  pay them the jews they want forget about  it because they will probably stop the  shipment and you won't be able to do  anything with it  and these are just a few examples of  what day-to-day life is like because yes  being an exporter in argentina is almost  mission impossible and the data  corroborates this argentina's economy is  one of the most close to international  trade in the world it's a country that's  decided to do exactly the opposite of  what the most prosperous countries have  done exactly the opposite strategy to  the one that once made this country one  of the richest in the world but having  reached this point it's over to you with  all these obstacles would you consider  investing in argentina what do you think  explains the great obsession against  foreign trade leave us your answers in  the comments below and now if you found  this video interesting don't forget to  like and subscribe to all of us here at  visualpolitik all the best and i'll see  you next time  [Music]

